2026-09-25|Maximum Height of Stack with Rotations and Multiple Instances

Question
- Given `height[]`, `width[]`, and `length[]`, find the maximum possible height of a stack.
- Each box can be rotated in any of the three ways.
- A box can be placed above another only when both its base dimensions are strictly smaller.

Approach
1. Generate all 3 possible orientations of every box.
- Each dimension can become the height.
- The remaining two dimensions become the base.

2. Normalize every base so that:
- `baseLength >= baseWidth`
- This makes comparisons consistent.

3. For every orientation, calculate the maximum stack height when that orientation is the bottom box.

4. Suppose orientation `i` has base `(L1, W1)`.
- We can place orientation `j` above it when:
  `L2 < L1` and `W2 < W1`

5. Use dynamic programming:
- `dp[i]` = maximum stack height with orientation `i` at the bottom.

6. Therefore:
  `dp[i] = height[i] + max(dp[j])`
  for every orientation `j` that can be placed above `i`.

7. Because both base dimensions must strictly decrease, we cannot form a cycle. Even though multiple copies of a box are allowed, the same orientation cannot be placed on itself because its base dimensions are not strictly smaller.
- So the same DP over the finite set of orientations is sufficient.

8. The answer is the maximum `dp[i]`.

Solution
class Solution {
    struct Box {
        long long h;
        long long l;
        long long w;
    };

public:
    long long maxHeight(vector<int>& height,
                        vector<int>& width,
                        vector<int>& length) {

        int n = height.size();
        vector<Box> boxes;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            // height = height[i]
            boxes.push_back({
                height[i],
                max(width[i], length[i]),
                min(width[i], length[i])
            });

            // height = width[i]
            boxes.push_back({
                width[i],
                max(height[i], length[i]),
                min(height[i], length[i])
            });

            // height = length[i]
            boxes.push_back({
                length[i],
                max(height[i], width[i]),
                min(height[i], width[i])
            });
        }

        int m = boxes.size();

        // Sort by base dimensions in decreasing order.
        sort(boxes.begin(), boxes.end(), [](const Box& a, const Box& b) {
            if (a.l != b.l)
                return a.l > b.l;
            return a.w > b.w;
        });

        vector<long long> dp(m);

        long long ans = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            dp[i] = boxes[i].h;

            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
                if (boxes[j].l > boxes[i].l &&
                    boxes[j].w > boxes[i].w) {

                    dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + boxes[i].h);
                }
            }

            ans = max(ans, dp[i]);
        }

        return ans;
    }
};
```

Example
- Suppose a box has dimensions `(4, 3, 2)`.
- Its possible orientations are:
  - Height `4`, base `3 × 2`
  - Height `3`, base `4 × 2`
  - Height `2`, base `4 × 3`

- For every orientation, we compare its base with the bases of other orientations and build the tallest valid sequence.

Key Point
- Rotating each box gives 3 possible orientations.
- After generating these orientations, the problem becomes a longest increasing/decreasing subsequence problem in two dimensions, where the value being maximized is the total height.

- Time Complexity: O(n²), because there are `3n` orientations.
- Space Complexity: O(n).
